id: BCC-3
title: Teach the contract rule to create tasks and outrank the CLI instructions
status: Done
assignee:
  - '@claude'
created_date: '2026-08-31 12:21'
updated_date: '2026-08-31 12:45'
labels: []
dependencies: []
priority: high
type: bug
ordinal: 3000
---

## Description

<!-- SECTION:DESCRIPTION:BEGIN -->
A real OMP session with MiniMax-M3 ("erstelle 3 neue Blogbeiträge") left the workflow entirely: the model ran `backlog instructions overview`, then `backlog task list --status "Todo" --plain`, got `No tasks found`, and from there planned in OMPs built-in todo tool and wrote files directly. All session counters were zero. Three causes, all in rules/backlog-md-contract.md: the rule has no branch for "no task exists yet" and never mentions `backlog_task_create`; Backlog.mds own multi-kilobyte CLI instructions outweigh the 636-byte rule and point back at the CLI; and the built-in todo list is the cheaper path, and under prewalk it is wired into a host feature, so the rule must position it as complementary rather than devalue it.
<!-- SECTION:DESCRIPTION:END -->

## Acceptance Criteria
<!-- AC:BEGIN -->
- [x] #1 The rule names backlog_task_create as the first step when no existing task covers the request
- [x] #2 The rule states that the backlog_* tools supersede the Backlog.md CLI instructions when available
- [x] #3 The rule relates the built-in todo list to the task complementarily, without discouraging it
- [x] #4 The rule stays under 1000 bytes
<!-- AC:END -->

## Implementation Plan

<!-- SECTION:PLAN:BEGIN -->
1. Add three sentences to rules/backlog-md-contract.md: backlog_task_create as the entry point when no task matches; the tools supersede the Backlog.md CLI instructions; the built-in todo list organises steps inside a task rather than replacing it.
2. Extend the prompts test so it pins all three statements and asserts the file stays under 1000 bytes.
<!-- SECTION:PLAN:END -->

## Implementation Notes

<!-- SECTION:NOTES:BEGIN -->
Three sentences added to rules/backlog-md-contract.md and three existing ones tightened to pay for them; the file is 963 bytes (`wc -c`), down from 1027 before the trim. test/unit/prompts.test.mjs pins all three statements and a separate test asserts the byte ceiling; 23 pass. The whole suite is 436 pass.
<!-- SECTION:NOTES:END -->

## Final Summary

<!-- SECTION:FINAL_SUMMARY:BEGIN -->
The contract rule now covers the three ways an OMP session left the workflow before it started: it names backlog_task_create when no task matches, states that the tools supersede the Backlog.md CLI instructions so `backlog instructions` need not be read, and places the built-in todo list inside a task rather than against it. Verified by test/unit/prompts.test.mjs, which pins each statement and the 1000-byte ceiling (963 bytes actual).
<!-- SECTION:FINAL_SUMMARY:END -->
</file>

<file path="backlog/tasks/bcc-4 - Continue-a-taskless-session-once-at-session_stop.md">
---
id: BCC-4
title: Continue a taskless session once at session_stop
status: Done
assignee:
  - '@claude'
created_date: '2026-08-31 12:21'
updated_date: '2026-08-31 12:45'
labels: []
dependencies: []
priority: high
type: feature
ordinal: 4000
---

## Description

<!-- SECTION:DESCRIPTION:BEGIN -->
B1-B3 are prose and only work while the model obeys them. Enforcement is the part that does not depend on obedience, and the machinery exists: OMPs `session_stop` fires when the model declares itself done, is awaited before settle, and accepts `{ continue: true, additionalContext }`. The existing turn_end steering only fires when an active task has open acceptance criteria, so it never covers work for which no task was ever created. Add a session_stop handler that fires when files outside `backlog/` were changed and no task is active; `deriveSession().pendingModifiedFiles` already carries the file list and `resolveActiveTask` the task state. Cap it at exactly one continuation per session in memory — the hosts cap is eight and would otherwise force the model onward eight times. Do not block: the user must be able to end the session.
<!-- SECTION:DESCRIPTION:END -->

## Acceptance Criteria
<!-- AC:BEGIN -->
- [x] #1 A session with changed files outside backlog/ and no active task produces exactly one continuation at session_stop
- [x] #2 A new session metric counts that continuation and the doctor reports it
- [x] #3 A second stop in the same session produces no further continuation
- [x] #4 A session with an active task produces no continuation from this handler
- [x] #5 A session with no file changes produces no continuation
- [x] #6 The handler never returns decision block
<!-- AC:END -->

## Implementation Plan

<!-- SECTION:PLAN:BEGIN -->
1. Add a session_stop handler to omp/index.mjs: continue once when deriveSession reports pending files outside backlog/ and resolveActiveTask finds no task, naming backlog_task_create and the files in additionalContext.
2. Cap it with an in-memory per-session Set, so the hosts eight-continuation budget is never the only limit; never return decision block.
3. Record a taskless-continue metric, fold it in deriveSession, and print it in the doctors per-session counter line.
4. Contract tests for the five cases: continuation once, metric counted, second stop silent, active task silent, no file changes silent.
<!-- SECTION:PLAN:END -->

## Implementation Notes

<!-- SECTION:NOTES:BEGIN -->
Four contract tests in test/contract/omp-extension.test.mjs drive the handler directly: one continuation with the changed file and backlog_task_create named, undefined on the second stop, tasklessContinues 1 in the journal, undefined with an active task, undefined with no file changes, and first.decision asserted undefined so the block path stays unreachable. The registered-events assertion now lists session_stop. 23 pass in that file, 436 in the suite.

Beyond the criteria: a session that used the Backlog tools and finished its task also ends with no active task, so the handler would have demanded a second task from the session that did everything right. It now skips any session whose journal shows Backlog engagement at all — a native tool call, a CLI mutation, or a resolved task id — with its own contract test.
<!-- SECTION:NOTES:END -->

## Final Summary

<!-- SECTION:FINAL_SUMMARY:BEGIN -->
A session_stop handler covers the case the end-of-turn steering structurally cannot: work for which no task was ever created. When a session ends having changed files outside backlog/ with no task active and no Backlog.md engagement in its journal, it returns one continuation naming the changed files and backlog_task_create, counts a taskless-continue metric that deriveSession, the doctor and the evaluator report, and never fires again in that session. It never blocks, so ending the session stays the users call. Verified by four contract tests in test/contract/omp-extension.test.mjs and the counter assertions in cache, doctor and eval tests.
<!-- SECTION:FINAL_SUMMARY:END -->
</file>

<file path="backlog/tasks/bcc-5 - Keep-an-empty-summary-from-overwriting-a-populated-one.md">
---
id: BCC-5
title: Keep an empty summary from overwriting a populated one
status: Done
assignee:
  - '@claude'
created_date: '2026-08-31 12:21'
updated_date: '2026-08-31 12:45'
labels: []
dependencies: []
priority: medium
type: bug
ordinal: 5000
---

## Description

<!-- SECTION:DESCRIPTION:BEGIN -->
writeSessionSummary in lib/cache.mjs overwrites an existing summary unconditionally. A session idle past ABANDONED_AFTER_MS (30 min) but still alive is swept by a newer session: its counters are frozen into a summary and its journal deleted. When it later ends properly, writeSessionSummary derives from the now-empty journal and writes an all-zero summary over the good one. Since the README clarification, zero counters mean "the model never touched the tools", so this is a false statement, not just a lost measurement. The resume case is unaffected: the sweep writes no summary for its own session id.
<!-- SECTION:DESCRIPTION:END -->

## Acceptance Criteria
<!-- AC:BEGIN -->
- [x] #1 A later call with empty counters does not replace an existing summary whose counters are non-zero
- [x] #2 A call with empty counters still writes when no summary exists yet, because that is a valid measurement
- [x] #3 A test covers both cases and fails when the guard is removed
<!-- AC:END -->

## Implementation Plan

<!-- SECTION:PLAN:BEGIN -->
1. In writeSessionSummary, treat an all-zero metrics object as a non-result: when one already exists on disk, keep it and return its counters instead.
2. Cover both directions in test/unit/cache.mjs — populated summary survives an empty write, absent summary still gets one.
<!-- SECTION:PLAN:END -->

## Implementation Notes

<!-- SECTION:NOTES:BEGIN -->
writeSessionSummary now treats all-zero counters as a non-result: if a readable summary already exists it is kept and its counters returned, otherwise the empty one is written as before. Evidence: two tests in test/unit/cache.test.mjs; with the guard neutralised (`if (false && emptyMetrics(...))`) the first fails and the second still passes, which is exactly the split the criteria ask for. 38 pass in that file, 436 in the suite.
<!-- SECTION:NOTES:END -->

## Final Summary

<!-- SECTION:FINAL_SUMMARY:BEGIN -->
An emptied journal can no longer overwrite counters that were already frozen. writeSessionSummary keeps an existing summary when the derived metrics are all zero, and still writes one when none exists, because that is a real measurement. Verified by two tests in test/unit/cache.test.mjs, checked against a deliberately disabled guard.
<!-- SECTION:FINAL_SUMMARY:END -->
</file>

