## Description

<!-- SECTION:DESCRIPTION:BEGIN -->
A `backlog init` run in a repository that already has a Backlog.md project overwrites `backlog/config.yml` with defaults, silently discarding project name, statuses, task prefix and every other setting. It happened in this repository during the BCC-2..BCC-8 verification and was only recoverable because the file was under version control. The guard chain already inspects every `backlog` shell command for quoting hazards, so the same chain can refuse this one call. Only an init that would land on an existing configuration is refused; an init in a repository without one must pass untouched. The refusal has to name the way out, otherwise a legitimate re-initialisation gets worked around instead of followed: move or delete the existing configuration to start over, or `backlog config set <key> <value>` to change one setting.
<!-- SECTION:DESCRIPTION:END -->

## Acceptance Criteria
<!-- AC:BEGIN -->
- [x] #1 A `backlog init` bash call is refused when the plugin finds an existing Backlog.md configuration for that directory
- [x] #2 A `backlog init` bash call passes untouched when no Backlog.md configuration exists
- [x] #3 The refusal names the existing configuration file and both ways forward: moving or deleting it, and `backlog config set` for a single setting
- [x] #4 Tests cover the refusal, the pass-through, and the wording
<!-- AC:END -->

## Implementation Plan

<!-- SECTION:PLAN:BEGIN -->
1. lib/bash.mjs: add an `initialisesBacklog(command)` predicate, anchored like `directBacklogCommand` in lib/quoting.mjs so only a real invocation matches.
2. lib/deny.mjs: add `initReason(configPath)` naming the existing file, `backlog config set <key> <value>` for a single setting, and moving or deleting the file for a deliberate re-init.
3. lib/integration.mjs: give `evaluateToolGuard` a bash branch — refuse only when `findProject(cwd)` reports an existing configuration, honour BACKLOG_MD_GUARD=0 as a warning like the write path, and count it as a guard event. Both hosts route through this one function.
4. .claude-plugin/plugin.json: add Bash to the PreToolUse matcher, otherwise the guard does not exist in Claude Code, where the accident happened. OMP needs no change: its tool_call handler already calls evaluateToolGuard for every tool.
5. Tests: unit tests for the predicate and the evaluator, contract tests through hooks/pre-tool-use.mjs for the refusal and the pass-through, and the manifest matcher test updated.
6. README: hook table row and the per-event cost paragraph, which now applies to Bash calls too.
<!-- SECTION:PLAN:END -->

## Implementation Notes

<!-- SECTION:NOTES:BEGIN -->
Guard chain (lib/bash.mjs initialisesBacklog, lib/deny.mjs initReason, lib/integration.mjs initGuard) and the Bash entry in the PreToolUse matcher were already in the tree; this run added the missing verification and docs.

initialisesBacklog is anchored like directBacklogCommand — start of command or after ;&| — so `echo backlog init` and a task title naming it do not match. `backlog init-something` does match (no such subcommand exists), which is the harmless direction.

Tests: test/unit/bash.test.mjs (predicate, both directions, non-string input), test/unit/deny.test.mjs (wording, config-set line checked with bash -n), test/contract/pre-tool-use.test.mjs (refusal through the hook, init without a project, other shell commands untouched).

README: PreToolUse row now names the init refusal. Validation: npm test 442/442, npm run lint clean, npm run typecheck clean.
<!-- SECTION:NOTES:END -->

## Final Summary

<!-- SECTION:FINAL_SUMMARY:BEGIN -->
A `backlog init` bash call is now refused when a Backlog.md configuration already exists for that directory, so it can no longer replace project name, statuses and task prefix with defaults. Both hosts route through evaluateToolGuard; Claude Code needed Bash added to the PreToolUse matcher, OMP already forwarded every tool. The refusal names the config file, `backlog config set` for one setting, and moving or deleting the file for a deliberate re-init. An init where no project exists passes untouched. Verified by contract tests through hooks/pre-tool-use.mjs (refusal, pass-through, other shell commands untouched) plus unit tests on the predicate and the wording; npm test 442/442, lint and typecheck clean.
<!-- SECTION:FINAL_SUMMARY:END -->
